  "worked_solution": "First, since $s=7({e}^{t}-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{7} & ={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ {e}^{t} & =\\frac{s}{7}+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{s+7}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{s+7}{7})) \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,3{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,6{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{s+7}{7})}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,6\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2s+14}{7},\\,\\frac{3s+21}{7},\\,\\frac{6s+42}{7}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s+14}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s+21}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+42}{7})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
